Once upon a time, a Gnome Warlock named Thaddeus was questing through the dense forests of Stranglethorn Vale. He had a powerful Succubus as his pet, a creature known for its speed, agility, and ability to summon additional damage. Thaddeus was confident that his Succubus would make short work of any enemies he encountered. As he ventured deeper into the wilderness, he came across a group of orcs harassing a merchant. With a swift cast of *Succubus's Shadow*, his pet transformed into a shadowy figure, striking down the orcs with ease. Thaddeus marveled at his pet's abilities, but he noticed that many other Warlocks in the area were using Voidwalkers instead. He wondered why, despite the Succubus's advantages.

The answer lies in the *Succubus's Shadow* ability. While it makes the Succubus a formidable companion, it also makes the Warlock invisible to the enemy. This invisibility can lead to the Warlock being seen as a threat less immediately, which can make the enemy less likely to attack. As a result, the Warlock may not need to use the Voidwalker as often, making the Succubus seem less common than it actually is. [end of text]


